|~ `::map` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusals of a payload it cannot read, and the words of maps. A map's
   elements are its values, and its keys are the shape that travels with
   them: a word whose answer names the map keeps the keys of the entries
   it keeps and answers under the tags of the map, and `map`, whose
   answer is any value, answers under none.
   The decisions it exists for: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:map
  |~~ A map holds named values under keywords, `{:id 7 :tags ["a"]}`:
      the value of a JSON object, a record of facts, and the shape an
      answer is written in. A value is read by its key, `/id`, a missing
      key a failure and `query /id` null; `keys` answers the keys as a set,
      and `entries` the pairs `[key value]`.

      It is written in braces, each keyword followed by its value,
      `{:a 1 :b 2}`, or as JSON writes it, `{"a": 1, "b": 2}`, whose
      keys read as keywords, so JSON pastes into a query as it is; one
      form a literal, and a key written twice keeps its last value.
      `::map{:a 1}` is `{:a 1}`. JSON writes a map as an object, and
      maps rank after sets.

      A map literal is the shape of an answer, each value computed
      against the subject, `{:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first}`
      answering `{:who "Ann"}`. `map ~(…)` runs a step on each value
      under its key, `filter ~(…)` keeps the entries whose value holds,
      and `toMap` builds a map back from its pairs.
      ::snippet~(::map | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::keyword | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps
        |~~ A map, read as itself. A map under a tag of its own gives the
            map beneath by `payload`, and the pairs of a vector build a
            map by `toMap`, each refused here.
            ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::vec/toMap | doc)
            ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::map` reads its payload as the map it is: the pairs of a
            vector build a map by `toMap`, `entries | toMap`, and a map
            under a tag of its own gives the map beneath by `payload`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isAMap |~~ Braces make a map. ~~| ~({:a 1} | type | eq ::map)
    | proves :buildsAMap
        |~~ The constructor of a map answers it. ~~|
        ~(::map{:a 1} | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :readsJson
        |~~ A JSON object is a map literal. ~~|
        ~("{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [true, null]}" | parse | apply it | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})
    | proves :keepsTheLastOfAKey
        |~~ A key written twice keeps the last value. ~~|
        ~("{:a 1 :a 2}" | parse | apply it | eq {:a 2})
    | proves :buildsBackFromEntries
        |~~ The entries of a map build it back by `toMap`. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | entries | toMap | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::map#1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesThePairsOfAVector
        |~~ The pairs of a vector are refused; `toMap` builds them into a
            map.
        ~~|
        ~([[:a 1]] | tag ::map !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::vec])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMapUnderATag
        |~~ A map under a tag of its own is refused; `payload` reads the
            map beneath.
        ~~|
        ~(::map::Box{:a 1} !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::Box])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/count
  |~~ Answers how many entries the map holds, `{:a 1 :b 2} | count`
      answering `2` and the empty map `0`, a map under a tag counting the
      entries beneath it. `count` takes no value: the entries to count
      are chosen before it, `filter ~(gt 1) | count`. A vector, a set and
      a string answer by a `count` of their own, a string its characters.
      ::link~(::map/empty | doc) ::link~(::vec/count | doc)
      ::link~(::string/count | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `count` takes no value; the entries to count are chosen before
            it, `filter ~(gt 1) | count`.
        ~~|
    | proves :countsTheEntries
        |~~ Three entries count three. ~~|
        ~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)
    | proves :countsNothingInTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map counts no entry. ~~|
        ~({} | count | eq 0)
    | proves :countsBeneathATag
        |~~ A map under a tag counts the entries beneath it. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | count | eq 2)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAPredicate
        |~~ A predicate given to `count` is refused; `filter` chooses
            first.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | count ~(gt 1) !| type | eq ::map/count/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds no entry, `{} | empty` answering
      `true`; it takes no value. A key held with a null value is an entry,
      so `{:a null} | empty` answers `false`. A vector and a set answer by
      an `empty` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/count | doc) ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the map holds no entry, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map is empty. ~~|
        ~({} | empty)
    | proves :failsOfAKeyWithANullValue
        |~~ A key held with a null value is an entry. ~~|
        ~({:a null} | empty | not)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/first
  |~~ Answers the value of the first entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, the order they were written, or null for the empty map; it
      takes no value. The entry itself, key and value, is
      `entries | first`, while `keys | first` answers the key first by
      name. The first entries of a map are `take 2`. A vector and a set
      answer their first element by a `first` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/last | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/first | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The value of the first entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `first` answers one value; the first entries of a map are
            `take 2`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheFirstValue
        |~~ The value of the first entry answers. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)
    | proves :followsTheOrderWritten
        |~~ The first entry is the first written, whatever its key. ~~|
        ~({:b 1 :a 2} | first | eq 1)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
        ~({} | first | eq null)
    | proves :readsTheEntryByEntries
        |~~ The first entry, key and value, is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:b 1 :a 2} | entries | first | eq [:b 1])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesACount
        |~~ A count given to `first` is refused; `take` takes it. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | first 2 !| type | eq ::map/first/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/last
  |~~ Answers the value of the last entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, or null for the empty map; it takes no value. The entry
      itself, key and value, is `entries | last`. A vector and a set
      answer their last element by a `last` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/first | doc) ::link~(::vec/last | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The value of the last entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :answersTheLastValue
        |~~ The value of the last entry answers. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
        ~({} | last | eq null)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the values, each a number, `{:a 10 :b 20} | sum`
      answering `30` and the empty map `0`; it takes no value. A value
      that is no number is refused with its key, and a field of each value
      is read first by `map`, `map /amount | sum`. A vector and a set sum
      their elements by a `sum` of their own, and two fields of one record
      add by `sumOf /x /y`.
      ::link~(::map/avg | doc) ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
      ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The sum of the values, `0` for the empty map. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ElementNotNumberError
        |~~ `sum` adds the numbers the map holds; `:key` names the first
            entry whose value is none, and a field is read first,
            `map /amount | sum`.
        ~~|
    | carries :ElementNotNumberError/key
        |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `sum` takes no value: a field of each value is read first by
            `map`, `map /amount | sum`.
        ~~|
    | proves :addsTheValues
        |~~ The sum adds every value. ~~|
        ~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)
    | proves :answersZeroForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map sums to zero. ~~|
        ~({} | sum | eq 0)
    | proves :sumsAFieldReadFirst
        |~~ A field of each value is read by `map` before the sum. ~~|
        ~({:a {:amount 1} :b {:amount 2}} | map /amount | sum | eq 3)
    | proves :ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "two"} | sum
          !| get [~(type) /actualType /key]
          |  eq [::map/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])
    | proves :ElementNotNumberError/refusesANull
        |~~ A null value is no number either. ~~|
        ~({:a null} | sum !| get [~(type) /key] | eq [::map/sum/ElementNotNumberError :a])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAField
        |~~ A field given to `sum` is refused; `map` reads it first. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | sum (get /a) !| type | eq ::map/sum/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the values, each a number, `{:a 1 :b 2} | avg`
      answering `1.5`, or null for the empty map, which has no mean, the
      mean of numbers staying among the finite doubles,
      `{:a 1e308 :b -1e308} | avg` answering `0`; it takes no value. A
      value that is no number is refused with its key, and a field of each
      value is read first by `map`, `map /amount | avg`. A vector and a
      set answer by an `avg` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/sum | doc) ::link~(::vec/avg | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The mean of the values, null for the empty map. ~~|
        #[::number ::null]
    | throws :ElementNotNumberError
        |~~ `avg` takes the mean of the numbers the map holds; `:key`
            names the first entry whose value is none.
        ~~|
    | carries :ElementNotNumberError/key
        |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :answersTheMean
        |~~ The mean of one and three is two. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 3} | avg | eq 2)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no mean. ~~|
        ~({} | avg | eq null)
    | proves :averagesTheOppositeEnds
        |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles and its
            negation is zero.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1e308 :b -1e308} | avg | eq 0)
    | proves :ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "two"} | avg
          !| get [~(type) /actualType /key]
          |  eq [::map/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/min
  |~~ Answers the least value in the one order of values, a number before
      a string, or null for the empty map: `{:a 3 :b 1} | min` answers
      `1`. A key the call gives, code run on each value, ranks the values,
      `{:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | min /n` answering `{:n 1}`,
      and a key under `desc` ranks in reverse. The entry of the least
      value, key and value, is `entries | min /1`. A vector and a set
      answer by a `min` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/max | doc) ::link~(::map/sort | doc)
      ::link~(::desc | doc) ::link~(::vec/min | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :key/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /n)`, it runs
            on each value, while `(get /n)` is computed once at the call,
            against the map, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The least value, null for the empty map. ~~| ::any
    | proves :answersTheLeastValue
        |~~ The least value answers. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)
    | proves :ranksANumberBeforeAString
        |~~ In the one order a number comes before a string. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "x"} | min | eq 1)
    | proves :ranksByTheKey
        |~~ The key ranks each value. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | min /n | eq {:n 1})
    | proves :ranksInReverseUnderDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` ranks in reverse. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1} | min ~(desc) | eq 3)
    | proves :findsTheKeyByEntries
        |~~ The key of the least value is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | min /1 | get /0 | eq :b)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no least value. ~~|
        ~({} | min | eq null)
    | proves :key/KindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A key written without its quote is computed at the call and
            refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:a {:n 1}} | min (get /n)
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/min/key/KindNotTakenError ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest value in the one order of values, a string
      after a number, or null for the empty map; a key the call gives
      ranks the values, as for `min`. The entry of the greatest
      value, key and value, is `entries | max /1`. A vector and a set
      answer by a `max` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/min | doc) ::link~(::vec/max | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :key/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null, `max /n`. ~~|
    | returns |~~ The greatest value, null for the empty map. ~~| ::any
    | proves :answersTheGreatestValue
        |~~ The greatest value answers. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)
    | proves :ranksAStringAfterANumber
        |~~ In the one order a string comes after a number. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b "x"} | max | eq "x")
    | proves :findsTheKeyByEntries
        |~~ The key of the greatest value is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | max /1 | get /0 | eq :a)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no greatest value. ~~|
        ~({} | max | eq null)
    | proves :key/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A key that is no quote is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | max 1 !| type | eq ::map/max/key/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ranksByTheKey
        |~~ The key the call gives ranks each value. ~~|
        ~({:a {:age 30} :b {:age 40}} | max /age | eq {:age 40})
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/map
  |~~ Answers the map of what the code answers for each value, under the
      same keys in their order, `{:a 1 :b 2} | map ~(add 1)` answering
      `{:a 2 :b 3}`. The code is a quote run on each value as its subject,
      not on its entry, so a key is read beside its value by
      `entries | map ~(…)`, and the pairs build a map back by `tag ::map`.
      The first error the code answers for a value is the answer of the
      call, and the answers stand in a map of their own, under no tag of
      the subject. A vector and a set answer `map` by words of their own.
      ::link~(::map/filter | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code run on each value, a quote. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `map ~(add 1)`, or a path, `map /name`;
            written without the tilde, `map (add 1)`, it is computed once
            at the call, against the map, and refused with what it
            answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ What the code answered for each value, under the same keys, a
            map under no tag.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ Each value is replaced under its key. ~~|
        ~({:x 1 :y 2} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:x 2 :y 3})
    | proves :runsOnTheValue
        |~~ The code runs on the value, not on its entry. ~~|
        ~({:a [1 2]} | map ~count | eq {:a 2})
    | proves :readsAKeyThroughEntries
        |~~ A key is read beside its value through the entries. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | entries | map /0 | eq [:a])
    | proves :answersTheFirstFailure
        |~~ The first error, in the order of the keys, answers the call. ~~|
        ~({:a "x" :b "y"} | map ~(add 1) !| get /trail/0/subject | eq "x")
    | proves :answersUnderNoTag
        |~~ The answers stand under no tag of the subject. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:a 2})
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesAStepComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A step written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | map (add 1) !| type | eq ::map/map/code/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/filter
  |~~ Answers the entries whose value the predicate answers true for,
      their keys kept, under the tags of the map:
      `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1)` answers `{:b 2 :c 3}`. The
      predicate is a quote the call gives, run on each value and not on
      its entry, so a test of a key reads the entries and builds the map
      back, `entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap`, and a key leaves
      by `minus`. The predicate answers a boolean; any other answer is
      refused with the key of its entry. A vector and a set keep their
      elements by a `filter` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/every | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::map/entries | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :predicate/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`, or a path,
            `filter /active`; written without the tilde, `filter (gt 1)`,
            it is computed once at the call and refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose values the predicate accepted, under the
            tags of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for the value of
            each entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered
            another kind.
        ~~|
    | carries :ConditionNotBooleanError/key
        |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ The entries kept keep their keys. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})
    | proves :keepsNothingOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map filters to itself. ~~|
        ~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box{:b 2})
    | proves :runsOnTheValue
        |~~ The predicate meets the value, not its entry: a number has no
            place.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | filter /0 !| type | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :predicate/KindNotTakenError/refusesAPredicateComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | filter (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/filter/predicate/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | filter ~(1)
          !| get [~(type) /key /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for every value,
      `{:a 1 :b 2} | every ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `true` for the
      empty map, which holds no value to fail it. The predicate is a quote
      the call gives, run on each value, and answers a boolean; any other
      answer is refused with the key of its entry. A vector and a set ask
      their elements by an `every` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/any | doc) ::link~(::map/filter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :predicate/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`, or a path,
            `every /active`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for the value of
            each entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered
            another kind.
        ~~|
    | carries :ConditionNotBooleanError/key
        |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :holdsOfEveryValue
        |~~ Every value is positive. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :asksTheValues
        |~~ A map is asked of its values, and one that fails fails
            `every`.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :holdsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map holds no value to fail. ~~|
        ~({} | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | every ~(1)
          !| get [~(type) /key /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])
    | proves :predicate/KindNotTakenError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call
            and refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | every (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/every/predicate/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for some value,
      `{:a -1 :b 2} | any ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `false` for the
      empty map. The predicate is a quote the call gives, run on each
      value, and answers a boolean; any other answer is refused with the
      key of its entry. A vector and a set ask their elements by an `any`
      of their own.
      ::link~(::map/every | doc) ::link~(::map/filter | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :predicate/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 0)`, or a path,
            `any /vip`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for the value of each
            entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another
            kind.
        ~~|
    | carries :ConditionNotBooleanError/key
        |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :holdsOfSomeValue
        |~~ One positive value is enough. ~~|
        ~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0))
    | proves :failsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map holds no value to pass. ~~|
        ~({} | any ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | any ~(1)
          !| get [~(type) /key /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])
    | proves :predicate/KindNotTakenError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call
            and refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | any (gt 0) !| type | eq ::map/any/predicate/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/sort
  |~~ Answers the entries ordered by their values in the one order, their
      keys kept, under the tags of the map: `{:a 3 :b 1} | sort` answers
      `{:b 1 :a 3}`. A key the call gives, code run on each value, ranks
      them, `sort /n`, and a key under `desc` descends,
      `sort ~(desc)`. The entries of the answer read in order by `entries`
      and `vals`, while `keys` answers its keys by name. A vector and a
      set order by a `sort` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/min | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :key/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null, `sort /n`. ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries in the order the key ranks them, under the tags of
            the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :ordersByValue
        |~~ The entries order by their values. ~~|
        ~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :ordersByTheKey
        |~~ The key ranks each value, and each value keeps its key. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n 2} :b {:n 1}} | sort /n | entries | map /0 | eq [:b :a])
    | proves :descendsUnderDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` descends. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 3} | sort ~(desc) | vals | eq [3 1])
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 3 :b 1} | sort | eq ::Box{:b 1 :a 3})
    | proves :key/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A key that is no code is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | sort 1 !| type | eq ::map/sort/key/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/take
  |~~ Answers the first entries of the map, as many as the count the call
      gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the map:
      `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2` answers `{:a 1 :b 2}`. A count past the
      size takes the whole map and a count below one takes none. The
      count is a whole number. A vector, a set and a string take by a
      `take` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/drop | doc) ::link~(::map/first | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/take | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :count/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2`; text that
            holds one is read by `toNumber` first,
            `take ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :count/NotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2`; a
            fraction is rounded first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(eq (floor))
    | returns |~~ The first entries, under the tags of the map. ~~| ::map
    | proves :takesTheFirstEntries
        |~~ Two entries of a map are its first two. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2 | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :takesTheWholeMapForACountPastItsSize
        |~~ A count past the size takes every entry. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | take 99 | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :takesNothingForACountBelowOne
        |~~ A count below one takes no entry. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | take -1 | eq {})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | take 1 | eq ::Box{:a 1})
    | proves :count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | take 1.5 !| type | eq ::map/take/count/NotIntegerError)
    | proves :count/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | take "1" !| type | eq ::map/take/count/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/drop
  |~~ Answers the entries after the first ones, as many as the count the
      call gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the
      map: `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2` answers `{:c 3}`. A count below one
      drops none and a count past the size drops every entry. The count
      is a whole number. A key leaves a map by `minus`, which takes the
      keys to leave out, `minus #[:b]`. A vector, a set and a string drop
      by a `drop` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/take | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/drop | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :count/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2`; text that
            holds one is read by `toNumber` first,
            `drop ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :count/NotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2`; a
            fraction is rounded first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(eq (floor))
    | returns
        |~~ The entries after the ones dropped, under the tags of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :dropsTheFirstEntries
        |~~ Dropping two leaves the last. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2 | eq {:c 3})
    | proves :dropsNothingForACountBelowOne
        |~~ A count below one drops no entry. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | drop -1 | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :dropsEveryEntryForACountPastTheSize
        |~~ A count past the size drops every entry. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | drop 5 | eq {})
    | proves :count/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | drop "1" !| type | eq ::map/drop/count/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A count that is no whole number is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | drop 1.5 !| type | eq ::map/drop/count/NotIntegerError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/reverse
  |~~ Answers the entries in the reverse order, their keys kept, under the
      tags of the map: `{:a 1 :b 2} | reverse` answers `{:b 2 :a 1}`. It
      takes no value. A vector and a set reverse by a `reverse` of their
      own.
      ::link~(::map/sort | doc) ::link~(::vec/reverse | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The entries in reverse order, under the tags of the map. ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :reversesTheEntries
        |~~ The entries come in reverse order. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ Each value keeps its key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | entries | first | eq [:b 2])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the values by the reducer, in the order
      the map holds them: `{:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add)` answers `3`, and
      the empty map answers the seed. The reducer is code: a quote of one
      word, by its name or its address, which the fold calls with the value so
      far as its subject and each value as its first arg, or any other
      code, which the fold runs on the pair of the value so far and the
      value, as a vector's fold does. A vector and a set fold by a
      `reduce` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/sum | doc) ::link~(::vec/reduce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :seed |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~| /0
    | takes :reducer
        |~~ Code: a quote of one word the fold calls with a value, or a path
            or a quote it runs on the pair of the value so far and the
            value.
        ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :reducer/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The reducer is a path or a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`, after the
            seed: written first, `reduce ~(add) 0`, it stands in the seed's
            place and hands the reducer's place the seed, and written
            without the tilde, `(add)`, it is computed once at the call;
            either is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The value the fold reached, the seed for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :foldsTheValues
        |~~ The fold adds the values to the seed. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 3)
    | proves :answersTheSeedForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers the seed. ~~|
        ~({} | reduce 7 ~(add) | eq 7)
    | proves :foldsInTheOrderOfTheEntries
        |~~ The fold meets the values in the order the map holds them. ~~|
        ~({:a "x" :b "y"} | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "xy")
    | proves :reducer/KindNotTakenError/refusesAReducerComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A reducer written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | reduce 0 (add) !| type | eq ::map/reduce/reducer/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :reducer/KindNotTakenError/refusesTheReducerFirst
        |~~ A reducer written before the seed hands the reducer's place
            the seed.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | reduce ~(add) 0
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/reduce/reducer/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :runsCodeOnThePair
        |~~ Code of more than one word runs on the pair of the value so
            far and the value.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1) | eq 3)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/keys
  |~~ Answers the set of the keys, each a keyword, in the order of their
      names and not of the entries: `{:b 1 :a 2} | keys` answers
      `#[:a :b]`, while `first` and `entries` follow the entries. It takes
      no value. A key written in JSON or read by `parseJson` is a keyword
      too, `{"a b": 1} | keys` answering `#[:"a b"]`, and a map under a
      tag answers the keys beneath it. The keys come with their values by
      `entries`, and one key is asked for by `has`.
      ::link~(::map/vals | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
      ::link~(::map/has | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The keys of the map, a set in the order of their names. ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `keys` reads a map: a vector of maps answers its keys element
            by element, `map ~keys`, and a vector's own places are its
            indices, read by `/0`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersASetOfKeys
        |~~ The keys are a set. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | eq #[:age :name])
    | proves :ordersByName
        |~~ The keys sort by name, whatever the order of the entries. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | first | eq :age)
    | proves :readsAKeyWrittenInJson
        |~~ A key written in JSON is a keyword. ~~|
        ~({"a b": 1} | keys | eq #[:"a b"])
    | proves :answersTheEmptySetForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no keys. ~~|
        ~({} | keys | eq #[])
    | proves :readsTheKeysOfEachMapByMap
        |~~ A vector of maps answers their keys by `map`. ~~|
        ~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | map ~keys | eq [#[:a] #[:b]])
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number has no keys. ~~|
        ~(42 | keys !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/keys/SubjectNotMapError ::number])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/vals
  |~~ Answers the vector of the values, in the order of the entries:
      `{:b 1 :a 2} | vals` answers `[1 2]`. It takes no value, and a map
      under a tag answers the values beneath it. The values with their
      keys are `entries`.
      ::link~(::map/keys | doc) ::link~(::map/entries | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The values of the map, in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `vals` reads a map: a vector is its own values, and a vector
            of maps answers their values element by element, `map ~vals`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheOrderOfTheEntries
        |~~ The values come in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | vals | eq ["Alice" 30])
    | proves :answersTheEmptyVectorForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no values. ~~|
        ~({} | vals | eq [])
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector has no values by key. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | vals !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/vals/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/entries
  |~~ Answers the vector of the pairs `[key value]` of the map, in the
      order of the entries, each key a keyword: the map with its keys as
      data, for a step that reads a key beside its value, as
      `get [~(type) ~(payload)]` reads a tagged value. `toMap` builds the map back,
      so a test of a key is
      `entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap`, and a key is spelled
      with its value by turning it to text first,
      `map ~(get [~(get /0 | toString) "@" /1] | join "")`. It takes no
      value, and the empty map answers `[]`.
      ::link~(::map/keys | doc) ::link~(::vec/toMap | doc)
      ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The pairs of key and value, in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `entries` reads a map: a vector of maps answers their pairs
            element by element, `map ~entries`.
        ~~|
    | proves :pairsEachKeyWithItsValue
        |~~ Each entry is a pair of its key and its value. ~~|
        ~({:auth {:port 3000} :db {:port 5432}} | entries | first | eq [:auth {:port 3000}])
    | proves :spellsTheKeyWithTheValue
        |~~ The key and its value spell one string. ~~|
        ~({:axios "^1.6.0"} | entries | map ~(get [~(get /0 | toString) "@" /1] | join "")
          | eq ["axios@^1.6.0"])
    | proves :filtersByKeyAndBuildsBack
        |~~ Entries filtered by key build back into a map. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :tmp 2} | entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector has no entries. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | entries !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/entries/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/has
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds the key the call gives, a keyword, a
      string, a number or a path, each naming the key as `get` reads it, a
      number by its text: `{:a 1} | has :a` answers `true`. A key held with a
      null value is held, `{:a null} | has :a` answering `true`, so `has`
      tells a null value from a key the map lacks, where `query` answers
      null for both. The key is computed against the subject, so it is
      written as the keyword that names it, `has :name`. A set asks for an element and a vector
      for a value by a `has` of their own, and a string asks for a part by
      `contains`.
      ::link~(::any/query | doc) ::link~(::set/has | doc)
      ::link~(::string/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The key the call gives, a keyword, a number, a path or a
            string, computed against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :key/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a keyword or a string, written as the keyword that
            names it, `has :name`, a number, which names the key of its
            text as `get` reads it, or a path, which asks whether it reads,
            `has /a/b`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the map holds the key, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :findsAKeyword
        |~~ A key held is found. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice"} | has :name)
    | proves :findsAString
        |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice"} | has "name")
    | proves :findsNoKeyTheMapLacks
        |~~ A key the map lacks is not held. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | has :b | not)
    | proves :holdsAKeyWithANullValue
        |~~ A key held with a null value is held. ~~|
        ~({:a null} | has :a)
    | proves :readsNoPathThroughAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A path through a value that has no parts does not read. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | has /a/b | not)
    | proves :findsTheKeyOfANumber
        |~~ A number names the key of its text, as `get` reads it. ~~|
        ~({:"1" 5} | has 1)
    | proves :key/KindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector key is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | has [1]
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::map/has/key/KindNotTakenError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/union
  |~~ Answers the entries of the subject together with those of the map
      the call gives, which wins a key both hold, under the tags of the
      subject: `{:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4}` answers
      `{:a 1 :b 3 :c 4}`. The values are not merged deeper; a key both
      hold takes the value of the other map whole. A set or a vector of
      keys unites with no map; a set and a vector unite by a `union` of
      their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus | doc) ::link~(::map/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The other map. ~~| /0 ::map
    | throws :other/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The subject unites with a map, `union {:b 2}`: a key comes
            with its value, and a set or a vector of keys alone is
            refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries of both maps, the other winning a key both hold,
            under the tags of the subject.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :letsTheOtherWin
        |~~ The other map's value wins a key both hold. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4} | eq {:a 1 :b 3 :c 4})
    | proves :mergesTwoMaps
        |~~ The entries of both are merged. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :takesTheOtherValueWhole
        |~~ A key both hold takes the other's value whole, not merged
            deeper.
        ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1}} | union {:a {:y 2}} | eq {:a {:y 2}})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq ::Box{:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :other/KindNotTakenError/refusesASetOfKeys
        |~~ A set of keys unites with no map. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | union #[:b] !| type | eq ::map/union/other/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/minus
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are not among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string, as `get`
      reads a key. `{:a 1 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp]` answers `{:a 1}`, and
      a key the map lacks changes nothing. A single key is written in a
      set, `minus #[:tmp]`. A set and a vector take out their elements by
      a `minus` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/inter | doc) ::link~(::map/union | doc)
      ::link~(::set/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :keys
        |~~ The keys to leave out: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
        /0 #[::map ::vec]
    | throws :keys/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys; a single key
            is written in a set, `minus #[:tmp]`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose keys are not among the keys, under the tags
            of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
        |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string;
            `:index` names the first element of the other value that is
            neither.
        ~~|
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfASet
        |~~ The keys a set names leave. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfAVector
        |~~ A string in a vector names a key to leave. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus ["a"] | eq {:b 2})
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ The keys of a map leave, whatever their values. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus {:b 9} | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :keepsTheMapForAKeyItLacks
        |~~ A key the map lacks changes nothing. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | minus [:z] | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1 :b 2} | minus [:a] | eq ::Box{:b 2})
    | proves :keys/KindNotTakenError/refusesASingleKey
        |~~ A key written alone is refused; a set holds it. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | minus "a" !| type | eq ::map/minus/keys/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | minus [:a 1]
          !| get [~(type) /actualType /index]
          |  eq [::map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/inter
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string.
      `{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b]` answers `{:a 1 :b 2}`, and keys
      the map lacks keep none of its entries. A set and a vector keep the
      elements they share by an `inter` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus | doc) ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :keys
        |~~ The keys to keep: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
        /0 #[::map ::vec]
    | throws :keys/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys,
            `inter #[:a]`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose keys are among the keys, under the tags of
            the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
        |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string;
            `:index` names the first element of the other value that is
            neither.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfASet
        |~~ The keys a set names stay. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAVector
        |~~ Strings and keywords in a vector name the keys to keep. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter ["a" :c] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ The keys of a map stay, whatever their values. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | inter {:b 9} | eq {:b 2})
    | proves :keepsNoEntryForKeysTheMapLacks
        |~~ Keys the map lacks keep none of its entries. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | inter [:z] | eq {})
    | proves :KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | inter [[:a]]
          !| get [~(type) /actualType /index]
          |  eq [::map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])
    | proves :keys/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ Keys that are no map, vector or set are refused under the
            input.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | inter 5 !| type | eq ::map/inter/keys/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/raise
  |~~ Answers the map as an error on the fail track, an error at rest,
      `!{:reason :late} | raise`, among them, so a query fails where its
      own check fails. Its tag is the one its value shows: the outermost
      tag over the map, then the one the map carries,
      `::Oops{:message "boom"} | raise`, then `::error`, every entry of
      the map a fact; and an error a catch opened, raised again, is
      itself. `|`
      steps around the failure, writing its trail, and `!|` catches it
      to its facts. It takes no value: what the error says is a fact of
      the map. A value of another kind is put in a map first,
      `get {:value ~(it)} | raise`.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The error the map is raised as, on the fail track. ~~|
        ::error
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `raise` raises a map: a value of another kind is put in one
            first, `get {:value ~(it)} | raise`, or the error is written as its
            literal, `!{:reason :late} | raise`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `raise` takes no value: what the error says is a fact of the
            map, `{:message "boom"} | raise`.
        ~~|
    | proves :raisesTheSubject
        |~~ `{…} | raise` raises the subject, a map the step builds among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({:message "boom"} | raise !| get /message | eq "boom")
    | proves :takesTheTagTheMapCarries
        |~~ The tag the map carries is the tag of the error. ~~|
        ~(::Oops{:message "boom"} | raise !| type | eq ::Oops)
    | proves :keepsEveryEntryAsAFact
        |~~ Every entry of the map is a fact of the error, a `:kind` among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({:kind ::Oops} | raise !| get [~(type) /kind] | eq [::error ::Oops])
    | proves :raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
        |~~ An error caught and raised again is itself. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| raise !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :renamesByATag
        |~~ A step that lays a tag over an error caught renames it. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| tag ::Renamed | raise !| type | eq ::Renamed)
    | proves :guardsAValue
        |~~ A guard raises an error of its own tag when a value fails its
            check.
        ~~|
        ~(42 | if (gt 100) ~(it) ~(get {:value ~(it)} | tag ::TooSmall | raise)
          !| get [~(type) /value]
          |  eq [::TooSmall 42])
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no map to raise. ~~|
        ~(42 | raise !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::map/raise/SubjectNotMapError ::number])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAMessage
        |~~ A message given to `raise` is refused; the map holds it. ~~|
        ~({:kind :oops} | raise "boom" !| type | eq ::map/raise/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:map/toMap
  |~~ Answers the map itself, so `map ~toMap` turns maps and vectors of
      pairs into maps.
      ::link~(::vec/toMap | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The map itself. ~~| ::map
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A map answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | toMap | eq {:a 1})
    | impl ::builtin{}

